  "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of bottles checked until a defect is found. On average, the chance of finding an upside down label on any water bottle is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{50}=0.08.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of bottles that the inspector checks before finding an upside down label can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.08.$\n\nSo, $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(0.08).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.08)^{x-1}(0.08) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{x-1}(0.08)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{5-1}(0.08) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{4}(0.08) \\\\  & \u22480.057\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
